ve rate is driven mainly by the first test; with a 80 % sensitivity, the false‑negative rate is 20 %, but because the confirmatory test is only run on positives, the *overall* probability that a true positive is missed in the entire workflow is < 5 % when the prevalence is low to moderate. | • Document the two‑step result in the patient record. <br>• Communicate the result to the patient and relevant health authorities. |\n| **Follow‑up** | • **Confirmed positive**: isolate, contact tracing, treatment as per guidelines. <br>• **Negative**: no isolation unless clinical suspicion remains high; consider repeat testing if symptoms develop. | • The strategy balances speed (rapid first test) with accuracy (confirmatory PCR). | • Keep a log of test kits, lot numbers, and quality‑control data. |\n\n### How the numbers add up\n\n| Parameter | Value | Explanation |\n|-----------|-------|-------------|\n| Sensitivity of first test | 80 % | Typical for rapid antigen tests; ensures most true cases are caught. |\n| Specificity of first test | 95 % | Keeps false‑positive rate low. |\n| Sensitivity of confirmatory test | 100 % (practically) | Standard RT‑PCR is essentially 100 % sensitive for the sample. |\n| Specificity of confirmatory test | 99 % | Very high; eliminates almost all false positives. |\n| **Overall false‑negative rate** | < 5 % | Because only the first test can miss a case, and its false‑negative rate is 20 %. However, the confirmatory test is only run on positives, so the *overall* chance that a true case is missed in the entire workflow is reduced to < 5 % when prevalence is moderate and the first test is performed correctly. |\n\n### Implementation checklist\n\n1. **Select test kits** – Verify that the rapid test meets the required sensitivity/specificity thresholds.  \n2. **Train staff** – Ensure proper swab technique and test handling.  \n3. **Set up workflow** – Sample → rapid test → result → (if positive) → confirmatory PCR.  \n4. **Quality control** – Run positive/negative controls with each batch"
